---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import Tree from "../../components/Tree.astro";

const H = {
  short: "What the reference OpenTofu sets up around the chart",
  how: "Modules, a production root and a bootstrap, per cloud",
  rules: "Each cloud's choices, checks and recovery",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Reference OpenTofu for Google Cloud and AWS makes what the chart runs on: the network, the
      cluster, the database, the files bucket, secrets, the pods' cloud identities and the VM tier,
      and on Google Cloud also keys, logs, an image registry and Binary Authorization. Each cloud
      has a production root that puts the modules together and outputs the chart values the cloud
      decides. They use the clouds' own providers alone, with no third-party modules, so a
      platform team can read every resource they make (<Adr n={[97, 107, 109]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <Tree dir="deploy/gcp/modules" depth={1} />
    <Tree dir="deploy/aws/modules" depth={1} />
    <p>
      Each cloud's <code>bootstrap</code> is made once, first: the bucket its state lives in and the
      key that encrypts it. <code>production</code> is one deployment, and its README is the runbook
      from an empty project or account to Iki serving. Its <code>helm_values</code> output is the
      chart's values for that cloud, written beside <code>values-production.yaml</code> (<Path
        p="deploy/gcp/production/outputs.tf"
        sym='output "helm_values"'
      />, <Path p="deploy/aws/production/outputs.tf" sym='output "helm_values"' />, <PageLink
        to="The Helm chart"
      />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Google Cloud" open>
        <p>
          Artifact Registry tags are immutable (<Path
            p="deploy/gcp/modules/artifact-registry/main.tf"
            sym="immutable_tags = true"
          />). Files use Cloud Storage's own API, and the VM tier runs on Compute Engine (<Adr
            n="0109"
          />). Binary Authorization admits only the deployment's repositories, Iki's images and
          listed extras, and denies the rest cluster-wide (<Path
            p="deploy/gcp/modules/binary-authorization/main.tf"
            sym="ALWAYS_DENY"
          />). Pods reach Google through Workload Identity.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="AWS">
        <p>
          EKS with Bottlerocket nodes; pods get credentials through EKS Pod Identity, one role each,
          allowed only what it uses (<Path p="deploy/aws/modules/access/main.tf" sym="aws_eks_pod_identity_association" />).
          FSx for Lustre is optional, for shared reference data.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Secrets never enter the state">
        <p>
          No module writes a secret's value, so none is in OpenTofu's state; <code>make check-infra</code>{" "}
          fails if one does. It also runs <code>tofu fmt</code>, <code>validate</code> and each module's
          tests against mocked providers, and Checkov, per cloud (<Path p="Makefile" sym="check-infra:" />).
          The real deployments are <PageLink to="Live clouds and scale" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Recovering from a lost region">
        <p>
          Both production roots take a <code>recovery</code> variable, set only to stand Iki up again
          in another region from the same code: the lost deployment's promoted database replica and
          its files bucket become this one's (<Adr n="0100" />, <Path
            p="deploy/gcp/production/variables.tf"
            sym='variable "recovery"'
          />, <Path p="deploy/aws/production/variables.tf" sym='variable "recovery"' />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0107" /> says files go through Cloud Storage's S3-compatible API; <Adr n="0109" />{" "}
            and the code use its own API, and 0107's status doesn't say so.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p="deploy/gcp/README.md" />, <Path p="deploy/aws/README.md" />: what each module makes</li>
        <li><Path p="deploy/gcp/production" />, <Path p="deploy/aws/production" />: the roots and their runbooks</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0097" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0100" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0107" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0109" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
